---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Workspace for Developers — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-indigo-on-dark">For Developers</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Workspace for Developers</h1>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-2">
                Your home is Workspace at the root URL. Administration is not this role.
                Hitting /Admin redirects home.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
            <h2>Current team and project</h2>
            <p>
                The sidebar footer holds the team switcher. Pick a team, then a project
                you can access. Cookies <code class="text-foreground">Querial.TeamId</code> and
                <code class="text-foreground">Querial.ProjectId</code> plus preferences
                <code class="text-foreground">ui.team</code> / <code class="text-foreground">ui.project</code>
                remember the pair. After a switch, the page reloads a clean path so stale
                pipeline or run ids are not reused.
            </p>
            <p>
                If a URL names a resource in another project you can access, cookies
                move to that team and project and the page continues. If you cannot
                access it, the response is <strong class="text-foreground">404</strong>, not 403.
            </p>

            <h2>Empty states</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>No team: ask a Platform admin to invite you.</li>
                <li>Team but no project placement: ask a Team admin or Project admin to add you.</li>
                <li>Developer on an empty project: stay in Workspace. You do not get a Create project CTA.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>What the nav hides</h2>
            <p>
                Agents, Git remotes, and API keys require CanOperate — they are not
                in Developer nav. Operators own remotes; this role authors the package
                tree. See
                <a href="/docs/devops-git" class="text-link hover:underline">Git and package remotes</a>.
                Users, AI configuration, and instance diagnostics
                require Platform admin. You will see pipelines, the SQL workspace,
                schema, packages (as a project member), and run history as a member
                — you will not see Retry on a failed run. You still author
                <a href="/docs/developer-retry" class="text-link hover:underline">step retry policies</a>
                on the pipeline and step editors.
            </p>

            <h2>First day in a project</h2>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">
                <li>Confirm the footer shows the intended team and project.</li>
                <li>Open Connections as read-oriented context: you need logical names and providers to bind steps. Testing and editing endpoints are operator actions.</li>
                <li>Create or open a pipeline draft. Author steps in modals; open SQL in the workspace when the editor needs room.</li>
                <li>Validate the graph (at least one structural root, no cycles) and publish when the contract is ready. Operators deploy versions, not drafts.</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>Cultures and theme</h2>
            <p>
                UI cultures: en, tr, de, es, ru, uz, ar (Arabic is RTL). Theme cookie
                <code class="text-foreground">ThemePreference</code>. Help for cron,
                step types, and packages opens as markdown in a modal next to the control.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
